\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Multi-path recall (a) versus the MosaicOpt algebraic approach (b). In (a) four independent channels produce ranked candidate lists and application-level score fusion decides the final ranking --- there is no equivalence relation over plans, so all optimization is heuristic. In (b) the user writes a single logical plan; the RBO enumerates equivalent rewrites under the equational theory, and the CBO picks a physical realization. The fusion policy is explicit in \texttt  {ORDER BY}.}}{6}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:multipath}{{2}{6}{Multi-path recall (a) versus the MosaicOpt algebraic approach (b). In (a) four independent channels produce ranked candidate lists and application-level score fusion decides the final ranking --- there is no equivalence relation over plans, so all optimization is heuristic. In (b) the user writes a single logical plan; the RBO enumerates equivalent rewrites under the equational theory, and the CBO picks a physical realization. The fusion policy is explicit in \texttt {ORDER BY}}{figure.caption.7}{}}
